The principal functions of the planning board is to help the county anticipate the future and, at the same time, to conduct current business in a manner consciously designed to achieve county goals. At all times, planning board members shall closely cooperate with the commissioner, department heads, elected officials and citizen groups in order to plan for the future and implement such plans. A broad range of responsibilities of the planning board shall include, but is not limited to:
(1) Maintaining and updating the county comprehensive plan;
(2) Developing such specific plans as may be necessary and desirable for industrial parks, neighborhoods, park sites and other community facilities;
(3) Assisting in the development of the capital improvements program;
(4) Encouraging public participation in planning and governmental affairs;
(5) Helping county officials, as well as neighborhood and special interest groups, define their needs and goals as a part of the planning process;
(6) Assisting county government in formulating a shortterm work program and projects that will satisfy county objectives; and
(7) Coordinating federal and state participation in local capital improvement projects.
Specific plans and programs of concern to the planning board shall include:
a. Roads, streets, highways and public transportation;
b. Flood or other pertinent environmental control;
c. Revisions of subdivision regulations;
d. Establishment of a housing inspection program, including minimum or better electrical, plumbing and other codes as outlined by the state;
e. Establishment of a licensing regulation for building contractors operating in the county;
f. Community appearance, including sign and billboard controls; and
g. Soil and water conservation, including sedimentation controls.
(Res. No. 97-006, § 5, 3-25-1997)

